WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.761
Here is an English request.

00:00:04.761 --> 00:00:07.523
Find the users who don't listen to Taylor Swift.

00:00:07.523 --> 00:00:10.284
It sounds exact.

00:00:10.284 --> 00:00:13.046
It is not.

00:00:13.046 --> 00:00:15.807
Does it mean a user who never played a Taylor song?

00:00:15.807 --> 00:00:18.568
Then Mickey is out, because he played her, even though he plays the Beatles too.

00:00:18.568 --> 00:00:21.330
Or does it mean a user who plays anything besides Taylor?

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:24.091
Then Mickey stays in.

00:00:24.091 --> 00:00:26.852
One query returns only Pluto.

00:00:26.852 --> 00:00:29.614
The other returns all four.

00:00:29.614 --> 00:00:32.375
And Pluto plays nothing at all, so should a user with no listens even count?

00:00:32.375 --> 00:00:35.137
The English does not say.

00:00:35.137 --> 00:00:37.898
Same sentence, different answers.

00:00:37.898 --> 00:00:43.587
The model cannot tell which one you meant, so it picks one, silently.

00:00:43.587 --> 00:00:49.276
The fix is to write the meaning down.

00:00:49.276 --> 00:00:54.965
That precise statement is the spec, and the only choice is where it lives: in the prompt, or in the SQL you write yourself.

00:00:54.965 --> 00:01:00.654
A vague prompt pins nothing, so the model is free to guess, and you cannot tell which guess you got.

00:01:00.654 --> 00:01:03.368
Picture the same request as two prompts.

00:01:03.368 --> 00:01:06.082
The vague one is a single line, so the model can write either query.

00:01:06.082 --> 00:01:08.796
The precise one numbers what the model must not guess.

00:01:08.796 --> 00:01:11.510
The engine.

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:14.225
The tables, and exactly how they join.

00:01:14.225 --> 00:01:16.939
The definition.

00:01:16.939 --> 00:01:19.653
The edge cases.

00:01:19.653 --> 00:01:22.367
And a step by step trace.

00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:25.081
That definition line is the one only you can supply, because only you know what you meant.

00:01:25.081 --> 00:01:28.991
Every numbered line blocks a guess.

00:01:28.991 --> 00:01:32.901
Drop the join paths, and the model joins on the wrong column.

00:01:32.901 --> 00:01:36.812
Drop the edge case, and it quietly loses Pluto, the user with no listens at all.

00:01:36.812 --> 00:01:40.722
The prompt is not long for its own sake.

00:01:40.722 --> 00:01:44.632
Each line removes exactly one way for the model to be wrong.

00:01:44.632 --> 00:01:47.854
And two readings is the easy case.

00:01:47.854 --> 00:01:51.076
One phrase over two tables gave us two queries.

00:01:51.076 --> 00:01:54.297
A real question joins five tables and carries several independent forks.

00:01:54.297 --> 00:01:57.519
Which join.

00:01:57.519 --> 00:02:00.741
Whether a null counts.

00:02:00.741 --> 00:02:03.963
What to count.

00:02:03.963 --> 00:02:07.184
Each fork roughly doubles the possibilities, so eight queries all run, and only one is the query you actually meant.

00:02:07.184 --> 00:02:10.406
The spec is the single path through the tree to that one query.

00:02:10.406 --> 00:02:13.877
You resolve those forks in one of three ways.

00:02:13.877 --> 00:02:17.349
Push: write every answer into the prompt yourself.

00:02:17.349 --> 00:02:20.820
Pull: let the model interview you, one question per fork.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:24.292
Or just write the SQL, which is already precise, because every fork is a choice the query has to make.

00:02:24.292 --> 00:02:27.763
All three land on the same query.

00:02:27.763 --> 00:02:31.234
What you cannot do is leave a fork open.

00:02:31.234 --> 00:02:34.706
Something always picks it.

00:02:34.706 --> 00:02:38.177
Either you, or the model guessing alone.

00:02:38.177 --> 00:02:41.915
Pin the definition and you have chosen one query out of many.

00:02:41.915 --> 00:02:45.654
The spec names the right answer.

00:02:45.654 --> 00:02:49.392
But the model still has to write the SQL and land on a query that actually runs.

00:02:49.392 --> 00:02:53.131
That is the next step: execution.
